Z||000000||Pretender||05-11-2000||03:16 AM||||is there a way to set the idel? or is it all controlled by the aicv?..thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fiasco||05-11-2000||03:44 AM||||IAC valve, pretty sure. (Idle Air Control)||||reg||1
Z||000002||KnightRyder31||05-11-2000||02:19 PM||||How do you change it though? I have a problem with my idle too, it idles to low. I cant get at the screw to tune it down. Is there any other way? Also, my radio keeps going out...is this a comon problem for the Fiero? I own a 84' coupe. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||05-11-2000||03:26 PM||||The minimum idle is set with a screw, but it is far below the ECM's settings. The screw is usually set in the 550 RPM range. Lope, lope, lope.....<P>The ECM then uses the IAC to meter air around the throttle plate to get the idle in the 700-900 range (the V6 target RPM is 900. I'm not sure on the 4 bangers. Likely somewhere in there).<P>If you adjust the idle up with the screw, you are allowing air to leak through the throttle area. This causes the IAC to close the idle passage down. Eventually you get where the IAC is completely closed. Then you get the IAC error code. Of course, any air leak will cause this same problem and throw the code.<P>If you drop the screw, the IAC is open more. It's harder to get this situation to throw a code since the IAC can just increase it's position and drag the idle up. At some point, the screw isn't doing anything. <P>So, if you are trying to adjust the idle with screw, it will cause other problems with the idle. If the idle is out of range (specified in the ECM calibration table) for a period of time, the IAC code will throw.<P>Too high is usually an air leak and too low is a clogged TB idle passage or crudded up IAC pindle (cone at the end). In either case, I would expect and IAC error to be thrown. Bad crankcase ventilation can cause problems too with idle.<P>TK<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fiasco||05-11-2000||03:41 PM||||Yeah, that's what I meant.||||reg||12
